 place value chart below for $7.2\\times 1000$.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ | **.** | $\\fbox{\\phantom{0}}$ |" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186054460 1071 "What factor is represented by the empty box in the following equation?\n$24 = 2 \\times 3 \\times {\\color{black} \\fbox{\\phantom{A}}\\,}$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186054460 1071 "What factor is represented by the empty box in the following equation?\n$A$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186054460 1074 "We know that\n\n$$\n2 \\times 3 = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be simplified as\n$24 = 6 \\times { \\fbox{\\phantom{A}}\\,}.$\n\nFurthermore, we know that\n\n$$\n24 = 6 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing factor is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186054460 1074 "We know that\n\n$$\n2 \\times 3 = 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation can be simplified as\n$A$\n\nFurthermore, we know that\n\n$$\n24 = 6 \\times {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing factor is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186060932 1064 "The **minor** of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a matrix $A$ is the determinant of the remaining entries when the row and column containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor instance, suppose we want to find the minor of $a_{{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}} = {\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1}}$ (row ${\\color{red}1}$, column ${\\color{blue}2}$) in the matrix $A$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the minor, we exclude all entries in row $1$ and all entries in column $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nA=\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{gray}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{3}}} & {\\color{gray}{-1}} & {\\color{gray}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{6}}} \\\\ 5 & {\\color{gray}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{2}}} & 7 \\\\ 8 & {\\color{gray}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{9}}} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis leaves us with the $2\\times 2$ determinant given by\n\n$$\nM_{12} = \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe call it $M_{{\\color{red}{1}}{\\color{blue}{2}}}$ to denote the fact that it was obtained by deleting row $\\color{red}1$ and column $\\color{blue}2$ of the matrix $A.$\n\nFinally, we compute the above determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =20-56 \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186060932 1064 "The **minor** of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a matrix $A$ is the determinant of the remaining entries when the row and column containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor instance, suppose we want to find the minor of $a_{{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}2}} = {\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-1}}$ (row ${\\color{red}1}$, column ${\\color{blue}2}$) in the matrix $A$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the minor, we exclude all entries in row $1$ and all entries in column $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cancel{3} & -1 & \\cancel{6} \\\\ 5 & \\cancel{2} & 7 \\\\ 8 & \\cancel{9} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis leaves us with the $2\\times 2$ determinant given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe call it $M_{{\\color{red}{1}}{\\color{blue}{2}}}$ to denote the fact that it was obtained by deleting row $\\color{red}1$ and column $\\color{blue}2$ of the matrix $A.$\n\nFinally, we compute the above determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =20-56 \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186060940 1064 "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186060940 1064 "Suppose we are given a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n\t\\qquad\n\tM =\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\n\t\\right]\n$$\n\nWe can apply some operations to the augmented matrix that do not change the set of solutions for the corresponding system. These operations are called **elementary row operations**. \n\nAfter applying an **elementary row operation** to the augmented matrix, the new corresponding system will have the same solutions.\n\nThere are three types of elementary row operations:\n\n- switching any two rows: \n\n$$\nR_i \\leftrightarrow R_j\n$$\n\n- multiplying (each element of) a row by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nR_i := k R_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- adding a multiple of one row to another row: \n\n$$\nR_j := R_j+k R_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\nIn the notations above, the \"$:=$\" symbol is called the **assignment operator.** We use this to assign new values to every element of a given row.\n\nFor example, the row operation $R_1 := 2 R_1$ means, \"create a new row $1$ by taking the old row $1$ and multiplying it by $2.$\" When we do this, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}:=2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 3 & 2\\cdot (-1) & 2\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA few final points on notation.\n\n- We use the similarity symbol ($\\sim$) to indicate when one matrix is transformed into another matrix using elementary row operations.\n\n- The assignment operator $:=$ is sometimes called the \"walrus operator\" because it looks like a walrus on its side!" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186061006 1064 "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\,7\\,)}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{11}}-\\underbrace{(\\,1\\,)}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{12}}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{13}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186061006 1064 "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\,7\\,)}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{11}}-\\underbrace{(\\,1\\,)}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{12}}+\\underbrace{(-3)}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}^{{M}_{13}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix}$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186073441 1074 "We must compare the size of the following product to the number $7{:}$\n\n$$\n7 \\times {\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{5}}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nis *less than* $1$, the number $7$ is *scaled down* (i.e., becomes *smaller*) when it's multiplied by\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\dfrac{4}{5}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is as follows:\n\n> *$7 \\times\\dfrac{4}{5}$ is $7$*" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] 