gin{aligned}3({\\color{blue}{0}})-{\\color{red}{2}} &=-2 \\\\ 6({\\color{blue}{0}})-2({\\color{red}{2}}) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2 &=-2 \\\\ -4 &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n  Both equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(0,2)$ is a solution.\n\n- If $x= 1$, we get the point\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  (x, y) &= (1, 3(1) + 2) \\\\\n         &= (1, 5).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n  This point is another solution to the system:\n\n  $$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3({\\color{blue}{1}})-{\\color{red}{5}} &=-2 \\\\ 6({\\color{blue}{1}})-2({\\color{red}{5}}) &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2 &=-2 \\\\ -4 &=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n  Both equations are satisfied. Therefore, $(1,5)$ is a solution.\n\nBy selecting any $x$-value and finding the corresponding $y$-value, $(x,y)$ gives a solution to the system and, consequently, a point of intersection between the two lines." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186079659 1064 "Suppose that we have an arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n3, \\: 9, \\:15, \\: 21, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nwith the common difference $d=6,$ and we're told that the sum to $N$ its terms is\n\n$$\nN\n$$\n\n \nTo find the value of $N,$ the number of terms in the sequence we are summing, we can use the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d\\big).\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute $a_1=3,$ $d=6,$ and $S_N=192$ into the above and solve for $N,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 3+(N-1)\\cdot 6) \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{N}{2}(6+6N-6) \\\\ 192 & =\\frac{N}{2}(6N) \\\\ 192 & =3{N}^{2} \\\\ 64 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we must take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative. So, we conclude that  our series has $N=8$ terms." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186079659 1064 "Suppose that we have an arithmetic sequence\