 of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\nT = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\nT = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![]