$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & 4 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & -2 & -1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNote that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and above.\n\n- A **lower triangular** matrix is a square matrix in which all entries *above* the diagonal are zeros. For example, the following matrices are all lower triangular matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 3 & 1 & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ -4 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ 7 & -1 & 1 & {\\color{blue}{0}} \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ -4 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ 4 & 0 & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ 7 & -1 & 1 & {\\color{blue}0} \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, note that we can have any number (including $0$) on the diagonal and below." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186121025 1064 "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 5 & 12 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\nM_{32}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186121025 1064 "The minor of the entry $a_{ij}$ in a $n\\times n$ matrix is the determinant that we get when the rows and columns containing $a_{ij}$ are removed.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to find the minor of $a_{32}$ in the following matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5 & 12 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nTo get the minor of $a_{\\color{red}{3}\\color{blue}{2}}$, we remove the $\\color{red}3$rd row and $\\color{blue}2$nd column:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ∗ & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 5 & 12 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ -5 & ∗ & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of $a_{32}$, denoted $M_{32}$, is \n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 12 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the value of $M_{32}$ by evaluating the determinant using previously discussed methods." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186121083 1064 "If a surface is parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ then the **fundamental vector product** of the surface is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v = \n\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂u} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn particular, if the fundamental vector product of a surface is nonzero at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ then it defines a normal vector to the surface at this point.\n\nLet's find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle \\sqrt{2}\\cos{u}, \\: \\sqrt{2}\\sin{u}, \\: v \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186121083 1064 "If a surface is parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ then the **fundamental vector product** of the surface is defined as\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂u} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{