 \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}| & | & |\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\end{bmatrix} \n\\:\\: \\right|.\n$$\n\nIt's much harder to draw a $4$-dimesional parallelepiped that is spanned by the four-dimensional vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4.$ However, we can define its volume similar to the ones above. It will be equal to the absolute value of the corresponding determinant:\n\n$$\nV = \\left| \\:\\:\n\\det\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}| & | & | & |\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & {\\mathbf{a}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}| & | & | & |\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\end{bmatrix} \n\\:\\: \\right|\n$$\n\nVolumes for parallelepipeds of higher dimensions are defined analogously." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186121131 1064 "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the matrix. For example:\n\n- The parallelogram (a.k.a. $2$-dimensional parallelepiped) spanned by the two-dimensional vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/c5/c538736c09cab15b58d7318c9c2361b2ae08e03b0f15231736d4b7b47f08ec59.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelogram is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,| & |\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,p & q\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,| & |\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the three-dimensional vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/7a/7ae38eb9a0d8e7a68b23dc40513f0c813cebf72c2bf839b196fd251d619b702b.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelepiped is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,| & | & |\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,a & b & c\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,| & | & |\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIt's much harder to draw a $4$-dimesional parallelepiped that is spanned by the four-dimensional vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,$ $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ $\\mathbf{a}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_4.$ However, we can define its volume similar to the ones above. It will be equal to the absolute value of the corresponding determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,| & | & | & |\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} & {a}_{4}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,| & | & | & |\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nVolumes for parallelepipeds of higher dimensions are defined analogously." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186125781 1064 "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}^{S}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186125781 1064 "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{S}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}^{S}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\longrightarrow \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}}y \\\\ {\\color{blue}{c}}x+{\\color{blue}{d}}y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}} & {\\color{blue}{b}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{c}} & {\\color{blue}{d}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186128889 1071 "Consider the matrix differential equation $\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186128889 1071 "Consider the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t).$ Which of the following vectors are solutions to this equation?\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{5t} \\\\ 5{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{x}_2(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}t+5 \\\\ {t}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix},\\quad \\mathbf{x}_3(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186128891 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables). \nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t) &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)-\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\