e can add them, multiply them by a scalar, etc). However, complex vectors have a few extra properties:\n\n- We can take the complex conjugate of a vector by taking the complex conjugate of each component:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Complex vectors have a real and imaginary part. This means that we can write a vector in the form $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{\\color{blue}a} + \\textrm{i}\\mathbf{\\color{red}b},$ where ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}} = {\\color{blue}\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{x})}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{b}} = {\\color{red}\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{x})}.$\n\nThe real part of the vector contains the real part of each entry, and the imaginary part of the vector contains the imaginary part of each entry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186153361 1064 "**Complex vectors** are vectors whose entries are *complex numbers*, such as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{C}^2.\n$$\n\n In general, $\\mathbb{C}^n$ is the space of complex vectors with $n$ entries.\n\nComplex vectors work mostly the same as real vectors (we can add them, multiply them by a scalar, etc). However, complex vectors have a few extra properties:\n\n- We can take the complex conjugate of a vector by taking the complex conjugate of each component:\n \n\n$$\n\\overline{\\mathbf{x}} = \\overline{\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}} = \\begin{bmatrix}(\\overset{―}{-1+\\text{i}}) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{3+2\\text{i}})\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1-\\text{i} \\\\ 3-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Complex vectors have a real and imaginary part. This means that we can write a vector in the form $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{\\color{blue}a} + \\textrm{i}\\mathbf{\\color{red}b},$ where ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}} = {\\color{blue}\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{x})}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{b}} = {\\color{red}\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{x})}.$\n\nThe real part of the vector contains the real part of each entry, and the imaginary part of the vector contains the imaginary part of each entry:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} \n= \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{-1}}+{\\color{red}{\\text{i}}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}}+{\\color{red}{2\\text{i}}}\\end{bmatrix} \n= \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{x})} +\\textrm{i} \\: \\underbrace{\\color{red}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\color{red}\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{x})}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186153365 1064 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$T =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nGiven a point $A(1,1),$ let $A'$ be the image of $A$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ How can we find the coordinates of $A'?$\n\nFirst, we create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $A'.$ Therefore, $A'(-1,-2).$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186153365 1064 "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$T =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nGiven a point $A(1,1),$ let $A'$ be the image of $A$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ How can we find the coordinates of $A'?$\n\nFirst, we create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containi