ists a bijection (i.e., a function that's injective and surjective) that maps $A$ onto $B$. Also, we know that\n\n$$\n\\big\\vert \\mathcal{P}((a,b)) \\big\\vert = 2^{\\mathfrak{c}}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the transformation $f: \\mathcal{P}((0,1))\\to \\{1,3\\}^{(0,1)}$ that, given $A\\subseteq (0,1)$, maps $A$ to the function $g \\in \\{1,3\\}^{(0,1)}$, where\n$g = \n\\begin{cases}1, & \\text{if}\\hspace{0.222em}x\\in A \\\\ 3, & \\text{if}\\hspace{0.222em}x\\notin A\\end{cases}.$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA = \\{0.2,\\, 0.5,\\, 0.99 \\}\n$$\n\nthen\n$$\n\\displaystyle g = \\begin{cases}1, & \\text{if } x \\in \\{0.2,\\,0.5,\\,0.99\\} \\\\ 3, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThis transformation $f$ is both\n\n- injective, since $f(A_1) \\neq f(A_2)$ wherever $A_1 \\neq A_2$, and\n- surjective, since each function in $\\{1,3\\}^{(0,1)}$ has a preimage in $\\mathcal{P}((0,1))$.\n\nTherefore, $f$ is a bijection from $\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\mathcal{P}((0,1))}$ onto $\\{1,3\\}^{(0,1)}$.\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\n\\big\\begin{vmatrix}\\{1,3\\}^{(0,1)} \\big & = \\big & \\mathcal{P}((0,1)) \\big\\end{vmatrix} = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{2^{\\mathfrak{c}}}\n$$" 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186173206 1064 "An **affine transformation** $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function that can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nUnder this notation, \n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$ represents vectors belonging to the input space, and\n\n- $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$  represents vectors belonging to the output space.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis an affine transformation because it can be expressed in the form $\\mathbf x = A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,$ as follows:\n\n