 \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the *Robin (mixed) boundary conditions*\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = 0, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x).$ Substituting this into the boundary conditions yields a linear system for the constants $c_1, c_2$ with coefficient matrix $A$:\n\n$$\nA = \n\\begin{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we have the following theorem:\n\n> The BVP has a **unique, trivial solution** $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions* (since $y \\equiv 0$ is *always* a solution to the BVP).\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186230214 1064 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = 0, \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the *Robin (mixed) boundary conditions*\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = 0, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$\n\nThe general solution is given by $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x).$ Substituting this into the boundary conditions yields a linear system for the constants $c_1, c_2$ with coefficient matrix $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we have the following theorem:\n\n> The BVP has a **unique, trivial solution** $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions* (since $y \\equiv 0$ is *always* a solution to the BVP).\n\nLet's see a concrete example." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false] [17592186230221 1064 "Recall that we are considering the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = 0, \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = 0, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ \n\nIf we define the matrix\n\n$$\nA = \n\\begin{bmatrix}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}(b)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthen we have the following theorem:\n\n> The BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\n**Proof:**\n\nSince $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are fundamental solutions of the equation, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny'(x) = c_1 y_1'(x) + c_2 y_2'(x).\n$$\n\n- Using the first boundary condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 \\left(c_1 y_1(a) + c_2 y_2(a)\\right) + \\beta_1 \\left(c_1 y_1'(a) + c_2 y_2'(a)\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nand regrouping the terms, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 \\left(\\alpha_1 y_1(a) + \\beta_1 y_1'(a)\\right) + c_2 \\left(\\alpha_1 y_2(a) + \\beta_1 y_2'(a)\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n- Using the second boundary condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha_2 \\left(c_1 y_1(b) + c_2 y_2(b)\\right) + \\beta_2 \\left(c_1 y_1'(b) + c_2 y_2'(b)\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nand regrouping the terms, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 \\left(\\alpha_2 y_1(b) + \\beta_2 y_1'(b)\\right) + c_2 \\left(\\alpha_2 y_2(b) + \\beta_2 y_2'(b)\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}({\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}(a))+{c}_{2}({\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}(a)) &=0 \\\\ {c}_{1}({\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}(b))+{c}_{2}({\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}(b)) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich we can write in matrix form as\n\n$$\nA\\,\\mathbf c = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf c = [c_1, c_2]^T.$\n\nBy the *Invertible Matrix Theorem*, the *only* solution to this system is $\\mathbf c = \\mathbf 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$ Since $\\mathbf c = \\mathbf 0$ implies $y(x) \\equiv 0,$ the theorem is proved." 13194139541356 17592186576411 true] [17592186230221 1064 "Recall that we are considering the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x) + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = 0, \\qquad a \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 y(a) + \\beta_1 y'(a) = 0, \\qquad \n\\alpha_2 y(b) + \\beta_2 y'(b) = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i$ and $\\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i, \\beta_i) \\neq (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ \n\nIf we define the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthen we have the following theorem:\n\n> The BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\n**Proof:**\n\nSince $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are fundamental solutions of the equation, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny'(x) = c_1 y_1'(x) + c_2 y_2'(x).\n$$\n\n- Using the first boundary condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha_1 \\left(c_1 y_1(a) + c_2 y_2(a)\\right) + \\beta_1 \\left(c_1 y_1'(a) + c_2 y_2'(a)\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nand regrouping the terms, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 \\left(\\alpha_1 y_1(a) + \\beta_1 y_1'(a)\\right) + c_2 \\left(\\alpha_1 y_2(a) + \\beta_1 y_2'(a)\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n- Using the second boundary condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha_2 \\left(c_1 y_1(b) + c_2 y_2(b)\\right) + \\beta_2 \\left(c_1 y_1'(b) + c_2 y_2'(b)\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nand regrouping the terms, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 \\left(\\alpha_2 y_1(b) + \\beta_2 y_1'(b)\\right) + c_2 \\left(\\alpha_2 y_2(b) + \\beta_2 y_2'(b)\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}({\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}(a))+{c}_{2}({\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}(a)) &=0 \\\\ {c}_{1}({\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}(b))+{c}_{2}({\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}(b)) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich we can write in matrix form as\n\n$$\nA\\,\\mathbf c = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf c = [c_1, c_2]^T.$\n\nBy the *Invertible Matrix Theorem*, the *only* solution to this system is $\\mathbf c = \\mathbf 0$ if and only if $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$ Since $\\mathbf c = \\mathbf 0$ implies $y(x) \\equiv 0,$ the theorem is proved." 13194139541356 17592186576411 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
